SOLID:
------

SOLID is a set of 5 Object Oriented Design Principles that helps in
	->writing clean code
	->Redude tight coupling
	->improve scalability
	->easy to test
	
S-Single Responsibility
0-Open/Closed
L-Liskov Substitution
I-Interface Segregation
D-Dependency Inversion

=============================
Single Responsibility:
----------------------
	A class should have only one reason to change.
	
Bad Example (Multiple Responsibilities)
class UserService {
    public void registerUser(String username) {
        // business logic
        System.out.println("User registered");
    }

    public void sendEmail(String email) {
        // email logic
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }
}


❗ Problem:

Business logic + Email logic mixed

Hard to modify or test independently

✅ Good Example (SRP Applied)

class UserService {
    public void registerUser(String username) {
        System.out.println("User registered");
    }
}

class EmailService {
    public void sendEmail(String email) {
        System.out.println("Email sent");
    }
}


✔ Each class has one responsibility

========================

2.Open/Closed Principle:(OCP)
--------------------------------
Software Entities should be open for extension and closed for modification.

Bad Example:

class DiscountService
{
	public int getDiscount(String type)
	{
		if(type.equals("STUDENT"))
			return 10;
		if(type.equals("SENIOR"))
			return 15;
		return 0;
	}	
}	

Problem here is for every new discount, we need a modification in the class.

Good Example:

interface Discount
{
	int getDiscount();
}
class StudentDiscount implements Discount
{
	public int getDiscount()
	{
		return 10;
	}
}
class SeniorDiscount implements Discount
{
	public int getDiscount()
	{
		return 15;
	}
}
class DiscountService
{
	int calculate(Discount discount)
	{
		return discount.getDiscount();
	}
}

Benfit is , we can add new feature without touching existing classes like StudentDiscount and SeniorDiscount.

==================

Liskov Substitution Principle:
-----------------------------
	A child class is usable whenever its parent class is expected.
	
Bad Example:

class Bird {
    void fly() {
        System.out.println("Flying");
    }
}

class Ostrich extends Bird {
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Cannot fly");
    }
}

❗ Ostrich breaks parent behavior

Good Example:

interface Bird { }

interface FlyingBird extends Bird {
    void fly();
}

class Sparrow implements FlyingBird {
    public void fly() {
        System.out.println("Flying");
    }
}

class Ostrich implements Bird {
    // no fly method
}

✔ No broken inheritance contracts

=============================

Interface Segregation Principle:
-------------------------------

Client should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use.

interface Worker
{
void work();
void eat();
}
//this is fine
class Human implements Worker
{
public void work()
{}
public void eat()
{}
}
//here Robot no need to use eat method.But we  have to force to use bacase of interface implementation
class Robot implements Worker
{
public void work()
{}
//no need
public void eat()
{}
}

Good Example:

interface Workable
{
void work();
}
interface Eatable
{
void eat();
}
class Human implements Workable,Eatable
{
public void work()
{}
public void eat()
{}
}
class Robot implements Workable
{
public void work()
{}
}

✔ Clean & relevant interfaces

==================

D-Dependency Inversion Principle:
--------------------------------

Depends on abstration ,not concrete classes

Bad Example:

class MySQLDatabase
{
	void connect()
	{
		System.out.println("connected to MYSQL DB");
	}
}
class UserRepository
{
private MySQLDatabase dbObj=new MySQLDatabase();

void save()
{
dbObj.connect();
}
}

Tightly coupled to MySql.

Good Example:

interface Database
{
void connect();
}
class MySQLDatabase implements Database
{
	public void connect()
	{
		System.out.println("connected to SQL Database");
	}
}
class MongoDatabase implements Database
{
	public void connect()
	{
		System.out.println("connected to Mongo Database");
	}
}
class UserRepository
{
Database database;

UserRepository(Database database)
{
this.database=database;
}

void save()
{
database.connect();
}
}

✔ Easily switch databases
✔ Perfect for Spring Dependency Injection

